::ALG2_U05_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+6 and g(x)=2x, solve f(x)=g(x). {
= x=6#Correct. x+6=2x gives x=6.
~ x=3#This divides too early.
~ x=-6#The sign is wrong.
~ x=12#This doubles the solution.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=9, solve f(x)=g(x). {
~ x=9 only#This forgets the negative square root.
= x=-3 and x=3#Correct. x<sup>2</sup>=9 gives both the positive and negative square roots.
~ x=3 only#This misses x=-3.
~ x=0 and x=9#These do not both satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of f(x)=4x-7? {
~ 4#This is the slope.
~ -4#This is not f(0).
= -7#Correct. f(0)=-7.
~ 7#The sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What is the x-intercept of f(x)=x-5? {
~ x=-5#The sign is wrong.
~ x=0#This finds the y-intercept input.
~ x=1#This does not make f(x)=0.
= x=5#Correct. Set x-5=0.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If a table shows f(1)=3 and f(5)=11, what is the average rate of change? {
= 2#Correct. (11-3)/(5-1)=8/4=2.
~ 4#This is the input change.
~ 8#This is the output change.
~ 14#This adds values instead of comparing change.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation finds intersections of f and g? {
~ f(x)+g(x)=0#This finds when the sum is zero.
= f(x)=g(x)#Correct. Intersections have equal outputs for the same input.
~ f(0)=g(0) only#This checks only y-intercepts.
~ f(x)g(x)=1#This is not the general intersection condition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>A height model gives x=-1 and x=8 for time. Which value should be used? {
~ x=-1#Negative time is not viable.
~ x=0#This was not a solution.
= x=8#Correct. Positive time is viable.
~ Both values#The negative value is not viable.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which representation is usually best for reading intervals of increase from a visual pattern? {
~ A formula only#A formula may help, but intervals are often clearest visually.
~ A single point#One point cannot show an interval.
~ A verbal title#A title does not show interval behavior.
= A graph#Correct. Graphs show increasing and decreasing intervals visually.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x+1, what is f(4)? {
= 9#Correct. 2(4)+1=9.
~ 8#This omits +1.
~ 6#This uses the wrong operation.
~ 12#This multiplies by 3.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If g(x)=x<sup>2</sup>-4, what are the zeros? {
~ x=2 only#This misses x=-2.
= x=-2 and x=2#Correct. x<sup>2</sup>-4=0 gives both the positive and negative square roots.
~ x=-4 and x=4#These are not zeros.
~ x=0 and x=4#These do not both satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature describes where a graph crosses the y-axis? {
~ x-intercept#That is where y=0.
~ average rate of change#That compares outputs over an interval.
= y-intercept#Correct. The y-intercept occurs when x=0.
~ domain#Domain is the input set.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x+2, which is larger at x=3? {
~ g(x)#g(3)=5, which is smaller.
~ They are equal#9 and 5 are not equal.
~ Neither exists#Both functions are defined.
= f(x)#Correct. f(3)=9 and g(3)=5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup>=x. {
= x=0 and x=1#Correct. x(x-1)=0.
~ x=1 only#This misses x=0.
~ x=-1 and x=0#The sign is wrong.
~ x=2#This is not a solution.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution is not viable for a length measurement? {
~ 5#Positive length can be viable.
= -3#Correct. Length cannot be negative.
~ 0#Zero may be possible depending on context.
~ 12#Positive length can be viable.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(2)=7 and g(2)=7, what is true? {
~ The functions are equal for every input#One matching input does not prove that.
~ Both functions have slope 7#Function value is not slope.
= The graphs intersect when x=2#Correct. Equal outputs at the same input indicate an intersection.
~ The y-intercepts are 2#This does not follow.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What does f(x)>g(x) mean on a graph? {
~ f is below g#Greater output means f is above g.
~ f and g are equal#That would be f(x)=g(x).
~ f has no values#This is not about domain.
= f is above g#Correct. f has larger y-values than g for those inputs.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If a graph crosses the x-axis at -2 and 4, what are the zeros? {
= -2 and 4#Correct. Zeros are x-values where y=0.
~ 2 and -4#The signs are reversed.
~ 0 and 4#This misses -2.
~ -2 only#This misses 4.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which calculation gives average rate of change from x=a to x=b? {
~ f(b)-f(a)#This is only output change.
= (f(b)-f(a))/(b-a)#Correct. Divide output change by input change.
~ (b-a)/(f(b)-f(a))#This reverses the ratio.
~ f(a)+f(b)#This adds outputs.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x<sup>2</sup>, what is f(3)-g(3)? {
~ 3#This reverses the subtraction.
~ 15#This adds the values.
= -3#Correct. 6-9=-3.
~ 6#This is only f(3).
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which input gives f(x)=10 for f(x)=2x+4? {
~ x=2#This gives 8.
~ x=4#This gives 12.
~ x=5#This gives 14.
= x=3#Correct. 2(3)+4=10.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best compares linear and quadratic growth for large positive x? {
= Quadratic eventually grows faster#Correct. The squared term dominates for large positive x.
~ Linear always grows faster#Quadratic eventually grows faster.
~ They always grow at the same rate#Their rates are not generally equal.
~ Neither can be compared#They can be compared by features and values.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup>+1 has minimum value 1, what does that mean? {
~ The greatest output is 1#This describes a maximum.
= The least output is 1#Correct. The graph never goes below y=1.
~ The only input is 1#Minimum is an output feature.
~ The x-intercept is 1#The function has no x-intercept.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature is most important when comparing viable solutions in context? {
~ Whether the answer is first alphabetically#That is not mathematical.
~ Whether the number is largest#Largest is not always viable.
= Whether the value satisfies context restrictions#Correct. Context restrictions determine viability.
~ Whether the expression is longest#Expression length is irrelevant.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=x-1, what is f(x)-g(x)? {
~ 0#This would mean equal functions.
~ x<sup>2</sup>-1#This multiplies.
~ 2x#This adds instead of subtracting.
= 2#Correct. (x+1)-(x-1)=2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L07_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which answer best explains an intersection point? {
= An input where both functions have the same output#Correct. Intersections share x and y values.
~ A point where only one function is defined#Both functions must be involved.
~ A point where outputs are always zero#That describes x-intercepts only.
~ A point where domains are empty#Intersections are points on graphs.
}

